  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="header-inner">
      <div class="intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">Agentic Software Engineering Lab · Paderborn University</p>
        <h1>Five Bits</h1>
        <p class="tagline">Five perspectives, one team, and a thoughtful approach to building software with AI.</p>
      </div>
      <button id="theme-toggle" type="button" aria-label="Switch to night mode" aria-pressed="false">☾</button>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <section id="team" aria-labelledby="team-heading">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Meet the team</p>
        <h2 id="team-heading">Five people, many ways to engineer</h2>
        <p>We bring together interests in infrastructure, security, machine learning, data, testing, and design. Here’s what each of us is exploring—and thinking about AI along the way.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="member-grid">
        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:Photos/Dhruv.png}}" alt="Photo of Dhruv" width="128" height="128">
          <h3>Dhruv Savani</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, 1st semester</p>
          <p class="bio">Interested in backend development, cloud infrastructure, and open-source tools. Before a coding session, Dhruv likes to reorganize his terminal setup. He sees AI as a useful assistant for boilerplate and exploration—but reviews its confident mistakes carefully. He expects developers to spend more time on system design, requirements, and reliability.</p>
          <a class="linkedin" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/dhruv-savani610/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Connect on LinkedIn</a>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI won't replace developers, but developers who ignore it will fall behind those who use it well.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:Photos/Harshil.png}}" alt="Photo of Harshil" width="128" height="128">
          <h3>Harshil Zadafiya</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, 1st semester</p>
          <p class="bio">Focused on cybersecurity, DevOps, and automation, Harshil automates small daily tasks—even when doing them manually might be quicker. AI can speed up scripting and log analysis, but generated code may bring insecure patterns or outdated dependencies. In an increasingly automated world, security and compliance belong in every engineer’s toolkit.</p>
          <a class="linkedin" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/harshil-zadafiya/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Connect on LinkedIn</a>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 Every line of AI-generated code is a security review waiting to happen.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:Photos/Jevin.png}}" alt="Photo of Jevin" width="128" height="128">
          <h3>Jevin Vaghasiya</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, 1st semester</p>
          <p class="bio">Jevin is interested in machine learning, mobile app development, and competitive programming. He learns concepts by explaining them aloud to a rubber duck. AI can help compare algorithms and approaches, but trying first builds problem-solving skills. He believes the engineering challenge will increasingly be deciding what is worth building and verifying an agent’s work.</p>
          <a class="linkedin" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/jevin-vaghasiya-07b39b24b/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Connect on LinkedIn</a>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 Code is getting cheap; knowing what to build and why is the skill that stays expensive.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:Photos/Pooja.png}}" alt="Photo of Pooja" width="128" height="128">
          <h3>Pooja Garg</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, 1st semester</p>
          <p class="bio">With interests in data science, user experience, and software testing, Pooja enjoys reading error messages—and often finds them more informative than documentation. AI can help write tests and explore data, but its output needs validation against real evidence. She expects quality assurance to evolve toward strategies that verify AI-written software at scale.</p>
          <a class="linkedin" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/poojagarg-/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Connect on LinkedIn</a>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 AI writes the code fast; tests decide whether anyone should trust it.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:Photos/Yasik.png}}" alt="Photo of Yashik" width="128" height="128">
          <h3>Yashik Khunt</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, 1st semester</p>
          <p class="bio">Yashik explores web development, UI design, and data visualization. One weekend-long bug hunt turned out to be a missing semicolon. AI can quickly prototype interfaces, but it cannot know a team’s users or make accessibility decisions on their behalf. He anticipates faster prototyping—and a greater premium on product thinking, quality, and maintainability.</p>
          <a class="linkedin" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/yashik-khunt/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Connect on LinkedIn</a>
          <p class="hot-take">🔥 Prompting is the new Stack Overflow: useful until you stop understanding the answer.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="future-of-se" aria-labelledby="future-heading">
      <div class="future-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">Our shared perspective</p>
        <h2 id="future-heading">The Future of Software Engineering</h2>
        <p>Across Five Bits, we see AI as a powerful collaborator for generating, explaining, testing, and automating software—not a substitute for engineering judgment. Its answers can be useful and still be wrong, outdated, insecure, or mismatched to the people a product serves.</p>
        <p>Over the next five to ten years, we expect more routine coding and deployment work to be automated. Engineers will spend a larger share of their time framing problems, designing reliable systems, guiding agents, and checking that their outputs meet real requirements.</p>
        <p><strong>Our common ground:</strong> understanding the code, prioritizing security and accessibility, and validating results with tests and evidence will matter even more as software becomes faster to produce.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer>
    <p>Built by an agent, supervised by humans. 2026 · Five Bits</p>
  </footer>

  <script>
    (() => {
      const root = document.documentElement;
      const button = document.getElementById("theme-toggle");

      function updateButton(theme) {
        const isDark = theme === "dark";
        button.textContent = isDark ? "☀" : "☾";
        button.setAttribute("aria-label", isDark ? "Switch to day mode" : "Switch to night mode");
        button.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(isDark));
      }

      updateButton(root.dataset.theme);

      button.addEventListener("click", () => {
        const nextTheme = root.dataset.theme === "dark" ? "light" : "dark";
        root.dataset.theme = nextTheme;
        updateButton(nextTheme);
        try {
          localStorage.setItem("upb-theme", nextTheme);
        } catch (_) {}
      });
    })();
  </script>
</body>
</html>
